<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zouaves</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1830–1962</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zouaves were light infantry regiments of the French Army, first raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1830 and named after the Zwawa, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cabyle">Kabyle</a> tribal confederation that supplied the earliest recruits. Later recruited mainly from European settlers and Frenchmen, they were famed for their baggy trousers, short jackets and fezzes. The name and uniform were copied by other forces, including the Papal Zouaves who defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the 1860s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zouaves.jpg" alt="Zouaves" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paul Louis Narcisse Grolleron; cropped by Beyond My Ken (talk) 23:35, 26 July 2018 (UTC) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zouave_-_Grolleron_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
